\section{Representation Volatility Analysis}
\label{app:cosine_volatility}

To test whether shallow layers produce more volatile representations, we measure step-to-step cosine distance at biased vs.\ corrected layers across all four models (\cref{tab:cosine_volatility}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Step-to-step cosine distance at biased (shallow) and corrected (deep) layers. Contrary to the initial hypothesis, shallow layers are \emph{less} volatile (ratio 0.37--0.73$\times$).}
\label{tab:cosine_volatility}
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer (type)} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{Std} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L2 (biased) & 0.186 & 0.097 \\
 & L14 (corrected) & 0.411 & 0.143 \\[2pt]
OT-7B & L2 (biased) & 0.077 & 0.080 \\
 & L16 (corrected) & 0.206 & 0.091 \\[2pt]
QwQ-32B & L2 (biased) & 0.037 & 0.064 \\
 & L60 (corrected) & 0.102 & 0.075 \\[2pt]
R1-32B & L10 (biased) & 0.067 & 0.050 \\
 & L63 (corrected) & 0.092 & 0.076 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Shallow representations are tightly compressed: consecutive steps cluster within small cosine distances (0.04--0.19), while corrected-layer representations spread more widely (0.09--0.41). The crossing rate bias arises not because shallow layers are volatile, but because a linear decision boundary at a shallow layer operates on narrow margins within compressed representations, where minor numerical fluctuations produce spurious threshold crossings. Deep layers produce well-separated representations where crossings correspond to genuine semantic transitions in the model's reasoning.


